#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""RG OS first-boot installer screen. Started by rgos-session before the ROMS partition exists.

Does nothing (exit 0) unless the boot partition carries /rgos-payload and no /rgos-installed.json (see installer.py).
Otherwise it opens on a welcome screen with two buttons, Install and Power off (D-pad up/down or left/right to choose, A to
confirm). Install first pops up a menu asking which file system the ROMS partition should get (FAT32, exFAT or ext4; D-pad to
choose, A to confirm, B to go back), then draws a progress screen on the framebuffer while installer.Installer works in a
thread; Power off leaves the card untouched. Without a usable screen there is nothing to choose with, so the install simply
runs (with the default, exFAT).

While installing, the screen is a fake "kernel exploit" console (green text scrolling on black, see ConsoleFeed): pure decoration,
nothing in it is real. It lasts 10 to 15 seconds at least and keeps scrolling until the real install has finished.

Exit status: 10 = installed, rgos-session reboots now; 20 = Power off was chosen, rgos-session powers the console off;
0 = nothing to do, or the person chose to boot RG OS without finishing.
On a failure the screen stays up: A retries, B boots RG OS anyway (the installer runs again on the next boot).

The menu moves like the RG OS launcher (uimotion.py is a port of its motion): the tiles pop in one after another, one focus ring
springs between them and breathes, the focused tile lifts and gets a shine sweep, embers drift up behind, the caption fades up
after each move, the format popup rises in, and screens crossfade into each other. The loop runs at full frame rate only while
something moves and drops to an idle rate when the screen settles; the CPU / GPU clocks are held up while the person is
interacting (uiboost.py, as in the launcher).
"""
import math
import os
import random
import sys
import threading
import time

